---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="About">
	<main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-b from-indigo-50/70 via-neutral-50 to-neutral-50 px-4 pb-16 pt-8 md:pb-24 md:pt-12">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl space-y-8 md:space-y-10">
			<h1 class="bg-gradient-to-r from-indigo-900 to-indigo-700 bg-clip-text text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-transparent md:text-4xl">
				About
			</h1>

			<article class="rounded-lg border border-indigo-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-indigo-50 md:p-8 md:text-lg md:leading-relaxed">
				<p>
					This is a placeholder about page—you can swap this copy for a real biography, credits, influences, gear list, or
					anything else that belongs off the homepage. The navbar above links here and back to Home so routing stays obvious
					during layout work.
				</p>
				<p>
					If you paste longer content below, typography from your global stylesheet (paragraph spacing, headings, lists) will
					still apply cleanly inside this card or in plain prose outside of it.
				</p>
				<h2>Dummy section heading</h2>
				<p>
					Whether you&apos;re mocking up a touring band microsite or a broader portfolio, separating “Shows” material from
					static bios keeps the codebase easier to reorganize later without hunting through one giant route.
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</main>
</Layout>
